# docs/dupes-allow — the meta-repo's duplicate-code CENSUS (WI-078; WI-276).
#
# Read by project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py (wired as docs/stack.ini
# [step:dupes]). Each non-comment line records ONE sanctioned duplicated block:
#
#     <fp>  a.py == b.py       fingerprinted (WI-276): a 12-hex block fingerprint
#                              then the file pair. Sanctions ONLY the block whose
#                              tokens hash to <fp>. Line-number-free, so it holds
#                              as the files grow — but sensitive to EVERY token in
#                              the block, so NEW or edited copy-paste between the
#                              same two files gets a different fp and is NOT exempt
#                              until a line is added.
#
# Listing the same "<fp>  a == b" N times sanctions N byte-identical copies of
# that block (count-aware): the (N+1)th identical copy still fails. A legacy bare
# "a.py == b.py" line (no fingerprint) coarsely exempts the WHOLE pair as a
# substring — kept working for backward compatibility, but this census uses the
# fingerprinted form exclusively (see WHY below). So this file is the allowlist
# AND the census: it records every duplicated block the repo currently sanctions.
#
# WHY a census, not a fix (owner ruling 2026-07-12, repo-review §1 M6 option (b)):
# the kit's F5 working agreement DELIBERATELY duplicates small stable helpers
# (_utf8_console, the declared-policy readers, argparse/exit boilerplate) so each
# script ships independently copy-able — a shared _kitcommon.py was rejected. That
# policy was unbounded and uncensused; this file bounds it. The gate now fails on
# NEW duplication (a block whose fingerprint is not recorded here), forcing a
# conscious census edit — including copy-paste GROWTH between an already-listed
# pair, the file-PAIR-granularity hole WI-078 documented and WI-276 closed
# (repo-review-2026-07-22.md M-5).
#
# DRIFT GUARD (WI-291): the WI-registry parser duplicated between schedule.py
# (CMP-004, the pure decision engine) and check_trajectory.py (CMP-001, the
# validator) is kept apart ON PURPOSE — schedule stays stdlib-only + self-
# contained (IF-053 fixes the arrow: the validator consumes the scheduler, never
# the reverse), so a shared wi_registry.py was NOT extracted (this ruling). The
# one real risk of two parsers — DRIFT — is closed without a shared module by
# tests/test_wi_loader_sync.py, which locks both load_wis to identical shared
# decisions (WI id set, hard/soft predecessor split, status, SR-refs, title).
#
# RESIDUAL (honest): the fingerprint is a 48-bit (12-hex) hash of the block's
# COMPLETE token signature, so two DIFFERENT blocks could in principle collide on
# it — a birthday-bound accident, astronomically unlikely at the hundreds-of-
# blocks scale a real census reaches. (WI-276 rework: the earlier hash covered
# only the block's first --min-tokens window plus its extent, so any edit AFTER
# that window — same prefix, same length — reused the recorded fp and slipped
# through; hashing the full token sequence closed that far larger hole.)
#
# MAINTENANCE: when you legitimately add F5 helper duplication, run
#   check_dupes.py --src project-trajectory/scripts   (prints the exact
# "<fp>  a == b" line to paste), or regenerate the whole census with
#   check_dupes.py --src project-trajectory/scripts --emit-census.
# When a decomposition removes a block below, delete its line so the census does
# not rot into permanent cover.

